Sec. 70401 increases the employer-provided child care credit rate from 25% to 40% (50% for eligible small businesses) and raises the maximum credit from $150,000 to $500,000 ($600,000 for eligible small businesses), with inflation adjustment after 2026. It defines "eligible small business" using a 5-year gross receipts test and allows credit for third-party intermediaries contracting with child care facilities. It clarifies that jointly owned or operated child care facilities qualify and directs the Secretary to issue regulations. Effective for amounts paid or incurred after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70402 makes up to $5,000 of the adoption credit refundable and adjusts dollar amounts for inflation, with a special rule for the refundable portion using 2025/2024 base years. It excludes the refundable portion from carryforward rules. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 70403 allows Indian tribal governments to determine whether a child has special needs for the adoption credit, treating tribes like States. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

Sec. 70404 increases the dependent care assistance exclusion from $5,000 ($2,500 for married filing separately) to $7,500 ($3,750). Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70405 modifies the child and dependent care credit's applicable percentage to start at 50%, reduced by 1 point per $2,000 of AGI over $15,000 (floor 35%), then further reduced by 1 point per $2,000 over $75,000 ($150,000 joint) (floor 20%). Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70411 creates a new nonrefundable credit under Sec. 25F for individual cash contributions to scholarship granting organizations, capped at $1,700 per taxpayer, reduced by any State credit. It defines eligible students (household income ≤300% of area median gross income, eligible to enroll in public elementary/secondary school) and requires organizations to meet specific requirements including serving 10+ students, spending 90% on scholarships, and not earmarking funds. It also excludes scholarship amounts from gross income under new Sec. 139K. Effective for taxable years ending after December 31, 2026.

Sec. 70412 permanently excludes employer payments of student loans from gross income (removing the January 1, 2026 sunset) and indexes the $5,250 exclusion for inflation after 2026. Effective for payments made after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70413 expands 529 qualified expenses to include elementary/secondary school tuition, curriculum, books, online materials, tutoring, testing fees, dual enrollment fees, and educational therapies for students with disabilities. It increases the aggregate limitation for elementary/secondary expenses from $10,000 to $20,000. Effective for distributions after enactment (expenses) and taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025 (limitation).

Sec. 70414 expands 529 qualified higher education expenses to include qualified postsecondary credentialing expenses—tuition, fees, books, supplies, equipment, testing fees, and continuing education fees for recognized postsecondary credential programs. It defines recognized programs and credentials including industry-recognized credentials, apprenticeships, state/federal licenses, and programs on state WIOA lists or VA WEAMS directory. Effective for distributions after enactment.

Sec. 70415 replaces the excise tax on private colleges' net investment income with a tiered rate: 1.4% for student adjusted endowments of $500,000–$750,000, 4% for $750,000–$2,000,000, and 8% above $2,000,000. It defines "student adjusted endowment" as assets (excluding those used directly in exempt purpose) divided by full-time equivalent students, and requires at least 3,000 tuition-paying students with >50% in the U.S. It overrides regulatory exceptions to include student loan interest and federally-subsidized royalty income as gross investment income, and requires reporting of student counts. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70416 expands the definition of "covered employee" for the excise tax on excess compensation within tax-exempt organizations to include any employee or former employee during any taxable year beginning after December 31, 2016 (removing the 5-year lookback limitation). Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70421 permanently renews and enhances opportunity zones with decennial designations starting July 1, 2026, repeals the Puerto Rico special rule (effective December 31, 2026), and limits designations. It modifies low-income community definitions, repeals contiguous census tract rules, and sets designations to last 10 years. It modifies capital gains rules: deferral until sale or 5 years, basis increases of 10% (30% for qualified rural opportunity funds) after 5 years, and fair market value basis after 10 years (or 30 years for certain investments). It adds reporting requirements for qualified opportunity funds (Sec. 6039K) and businesses (Sec. 6039L), penalties (Sec. 6726), and appropriates $15,000,000 for IRS reports through September 30, 2028. Effective generally for amounts invested after December 31, 2026.

Sec. 70422 permanently increases the state housing credit ceiling by 12% (from 1.125 to 1.12 multiplier) for calendar years after 2025. It modifies the tax-exempt bond financing requirement to allow 25% financing if obligations are issued after December 31, 2025 and provide at least 5% of basis, or 50% financing. Effective for buildings placed in service in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70423 permanently extends the New Markets Tax Credit at $5 billion for each calendar year after 2019 and modifies carryover rules to limit carryforward to 5 years, treating pre-2026 excess as occurring in 2025. Effective for calendar years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70424 permanently reinstates and expands the partial charitable deduction for non-itemizers, increasing the limit from $300 ($600 joint) to $1,000 ($2,000 joint) and removing the 2021 expiration. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70425 imposes a 0.5% floor on individual charitable contributions, applied in order to specific contribution categories, with carryforward rules for amounts disallowed by the floor. It modifies the 60% cash contribution limitation and coordinates with other limitations. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70426 imposes a 1% floor on corporate charitable contributions (exceeding 1% of taxable income) while maintaining the 10% ceiling, with 5-year carryforward rules and special NOL rules. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70427 permanently increases the limitation on the cover over of distilled spirits tax from $13.25 to $13.50 per proof gallon. Effective for distilled spirits brought into the U.S. after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70428 treats nonprofit community development activities related to fisheries in the Bering Sea and Aleutian Islands as substantially related to tax-exempt purposes for entities identified in section 305(i)(1)(D) of the Magnuson-Stevens Act. It allows tax-free transfer of subsidiary assets to such entities within 18 months and exempts income from transferred trade or business. Effective on enactment and remains effective during the western Alaska community development quota program.

Sec. 70429 increases the charitable deduction for expenses incurred in support of Native Alaskan subsistence whaling from $10,000 to $50,000. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70430 expands the exception to the percentage of completion method for residential construction contracts (replacing "home construction contract"), allowing the exception for contracts estimated to take 3 years or less (instead of 2 years) for non-home residential contracts. It applies the exception for AMT purposes and redesignates provisions. Effective for contracts entered into in taxable years beginning after enactment.

Sec. 70431 expands the qualified small business stock gain exclusion: 50% exclusion for stock acquired on or before enactment held >5 years; for stock acquired after enactment, 50% at 3 years, 75% at 4 years, 100% at 5+ years. It increases the per-issuer limitation to $15,000,000 for post-enactment stock (indexed after 2026) and $10,000,000 for pre-enactment stock, with separate return limits of $5,000,000. It increases the gross assets limit from $50,000,000 to $75,000,000 (indexed after 2026). Effective for taxable years beginning after enactment (stock issued after enactment for gross assets).

Sec. 70432 repeals the American Rescue Plan Act's revision to de minimis rules for third-party network transactions, reinstating the prior $20,000/200 transaction threshold for Form 1099-K reporting. It applies the de minimis rule to backup withholding, requiring reporting only if transactions exceed both the dollar and number thresholds, with an exception if prior year payments were reportable. Effective as if included in ARPA (retroactive) and for calendar years beginning after December 31, 2024 (backup withholding).

Sec. 70433 increases the information reporting threshold under section 6041 from $600 to $2,000, indexed for inflation after 2026. It applies the threshold to reporting on remuneration for services and backup withholding. Effective for payments made after December 31, 2025.

Sec. 70434 allows the election to treat costs of qualified sound recording productions as expenses under section 181, with a $150,000 limit per production. It defines qualified sound recording production as a sound recording produced and recorded in the U.S., denies other deductions/amortization, and allows bonus depreciation (placed in service at initial release or broadcast). Effective for productions commencing in taxable years ending after enactment.

Sec. 70435 creates a new exclusion under Sec. 139L for 25% of interest received by qualified lenders on qualified real estate loans secured by rural or agricultural real estate (including aquaculture facilities and fishing/seafood processing property). It defines qualified lenders (FDIC-insured banks, insurance companies, certain U.S.-organized entities, Farm Credit instrumentalities) and qualified loans (made after enactment, not to specified foreign entities, excluding refinancings of pre-enactment loans). It coordinates with section 265. Effective for taxable years ending after enactment.

Sec. 70436 reduces transfer and making taxes for firearms: $200 for machineguns/destructive devices, $0 for all other firearms. It conforms section 4182(a) to deem $0-tax firearms as tax-paid. Effective for calendar quarters beginning more than 90 days after enactment.

Sec. 70437 creates a new Sec. 1062 allowing taxpayers to elect to pay tax on gain from sale of qualified farmland property to qualified farmers in 4 equal installments. It defines qualified farmland property (real property used/leased for farming for 10 years, subject to a 10-year covenant restricting use to farming) and qualified farmer (individual actively engaged in farming). It includes acceleration rules, proration of deficiencies, and election procedures (including at partner/shareholder level). Effective for sales or exchanges in taxable years beginning after enactment.

Sec. 70438 extends the disaster-related personal casualty loss rules under section 304(b) of the Taxpayer Certainty and Disaster Tax Relief Act of 2020 by substituting the enactment date for "the date of the enactment of this Act" in section 301 of such Act.

Sec. 70439 restores the taxable REIT subsidiary asset test by increasing the limit from 20% to 25% of a REIT's total assets. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025. [end of text]


